< The world grows three kinds of body on its own, and each wants something different: a **conclave** (the small flock of a faith — the faith ladder's `cult` standing, never villainy) wants recognition and growth and acts as its god's character leans; a **gang** wants coin and safety and thrives where the town's law looks away; a **beast** pack wants food and territory, is hostile to everyone, and breathes with the seasons. None appears from nothing: each is born from a fact the world already holds — a town's quarter spilling out, a den's holder, the wild in its season, a routed levy's deserters, migrants turned away — and its card says which. They move on the world's ONE road ladder like everyone — its lowest rungs are the hidden paths through forest, marsh and mountain that the map draws and use wears in — and each week a body CHOOSES road or path (a hunted gang leans to the path, a welcome flock to the road, a hungry winter pack comes down onto the road), sorties onto the road at the junctions, and lives off what it takes from the villages, hamlets and thorps whose walls and watch are thin (a walled town is no prey until its walls are breached) [CURED 2026-10-19 (fp-final r5) — B.1 · B.3 · B.4]; supplies are their readiness, their size and their very existence, and a held site is their home base and cohesion. They meet each other, the patrols, the caravans, the villages and the armies, and every such engagement is a hearing in its week. Their presence is what an embattled region is made of — the gauge stays the one gauge, the bodies feed it — so killing them makes a region QUIET, while only fixing what breeds them (the watch, the wages, the keep, the known trails, the bread) makes it SAFE. A body has no power of its own: its deeds move the standings of the towns, the courts, the faiths and the nobles who hold the sword. The engine never sends a body toward the party, never names a body's purpose, and never turns one into a menace; the DM may promote any body into a menace with one act, and may end, pin or re-stance any of them.
