< Over a body's life [CURED 2026-10-18 (fp-final r4) — L-BD 29–49 and the addenda: the evening words in one paragraph]: a small body is NAMELESS ("brigands on the Tolle road") until it grows past the size at which it could raid a hamlet; then it takes a name from its place and kind, towns begin to keep word of it, and a court may post a BOUNTY on it — the call board's hook for the table's heroes and villains. A peaceable flock ASKS ALMS of every town in its reach and gets what the town's god and purse allow; it may take an empty site or clear a brigands' den without being pursued, and a court that wants the place back may buy it out. Gangs and flocks cannot mend a fort past a modest state on their own; beasts never mend anything. Every body LEARNS (the stretch where it was caught, the village that paid, the riders' rhythm), and so do the towns and their riders — the habitual one is caught or evaded. A court's public-security office may lead a HUNT; a warden who holds that office knows the hidden ways of its land. A gang has ONE end: to scatter rich, to found a company of swords or a guild where none exists (far from where it is known), or to make a hamlet of the site it held so long; a guild war is what happens when it tries to overthrow the guild that already rules the quarter. A foreign court may SPONSOR a gang or a flock in secret — by a courier, a smuggler's load, a cache or a mother temple's alms — and the day the silver is found, the blame, the casus and the hunt all turn on the sponsor. A captive taken by a body — a gang's, or a hostile flock's when its god allows — is RACED for: the body that survives the ransom's clock and is paid keeps the price; the body beaten first loses the captive free; a body neither paid nor beaten lets him go [CURED 2026-10-19 (fp-final r5) — B.2 · B.7]. Every one of these is a card the DM may open any week until it resolves on its own, and every act drives the crises the world already knows — never a new one.
